Constraints.Constraint:
The guard's exact bytes, so the plan inherits decisions and not questions. The `antiOccupancyFailures` docblock gains this paragraph verbatim after the existing "Shapes the scan cannot prove are reported, never passed." sentence: *"The guard's threat model is accidental drift, not an adversarial construction: the next flush re-pinning a count as a literal, restoring an occupancy table, or adding a module-scope constant. Against that it holds. Known boundary, stated rather than hidden: eight deliberate constructions put a number back in silence — a module-scope alias whose numeric binding is never referenced from the body, a module-scope function returning the number, a class static property, an enum member, a BigInt literal, a field typed through a type alias, a computed property name, and a union-typed field — and resisting a deliberate construction is outside this guard's charter (D39)."* `visitSubtree` becomes: *"if (visit(node) === true) return true; return ts.forEachChild(node, (child) => visitSubtree(child, visit));"* with the signature `(node: ts.Node, visit: (child: ts.Node) => boolean | void): boolean | undefined`, and `pairingCallbackBody`'s callback returns `true` immediately once `found` is set: the shipped combined guard `if (found !== undefined || !ts.isCallExpression(node)) return;` is split into `if (found !== undefined) return true;` and then `if (!ts.isCallExpression(node)) return;`, and the callback ends `found = callback.body; return true;` — so a node that is not an `it` call still returns void and the walk continues, and the `true` fires only on the located binding. Every other caller of `visitSubtree` returns void, so its traversal is byte-for-byte unchanged. The two self-directed directions land in `src/artifact/xml.test.ts`: one reads the file, slices the docblock from the `Regression guard against the occupancy pins` marker to its closing `*/`, and asserts the threat-model sentence, the phrase "Known boundary", and each of the eight shape names; one builds `"const a = 1;
const b = 2;
const TARGET = 3;
const c = 4;"`, walks it with a visitor that collects identifier texts and returns `true` at `TARGET`, and asserts `["a", "b", "TARGET"]`, then walks again with a void visitor and asserts `["a", "b", "TARGET", "c"]`.
